<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  LC-134: macOS getUserMedia permission hook (WKWebView slice).

  This file is auto-merged into the bundle's generated Info.plist by
  tauri-bundler at `cargo tauri build` time (it looks for an `Info.plist`
  next to tauri.conf.json). It only takes effect once the macOS build path
  lands under LC-126 (currently parked for lack of macOS hardware + Apple
  credentials); on Linux/Windows builds the file is ignored.

  Why this is the macOS slice of the work:

  * The WebKit-layer getUserMedia consent is already auto-granted by wry's
    WryWebViewUIDelegate, which implements
    `webView:requestMediaCapturePermissionForOrigin:initiatedByFrame:type:decisionHandler:`
    and unconditionally calls the decision handler with WKPermissionDecision::Grant
    (wry 0.55, src/wkwebview/class/wry_web_view_ui_delegate.rs). So there is no
    Rust-side delegate for us to install - see install_media_permission_handler
    in src/main.rs.

  * What is NOT automatic is the OS-level (TCC) prompt. WKWebView reaches the
    camera/microphone through AVFoundation in the host app's process; macOS
    refuses that access - silently denying the stream, or terminating the app -
    unless the host bundle declares a usage-description string. With these keys
    present, macOS shows exactly ONE prompt the first time a 1:1 call requests
    camera/mic and remembers the choice in the TCC database across launches,
    satisfying the LC-134 acceptance criteria ("exactly ONE OS-level permission
    prompt, never a silent denial" + "remembered across app launches").

  The matching hardened-runtime entitlements (com.apple.security.device.camera
  and com.apple.security.device.audio-input) live in lets-chat.entitlements,
  referenced from tauri.conf.json's bundle.macOS.entitlements; they are applied
  by the codesign step owned by LC-126.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>lets-chat needs camera access to make video calls.</string>
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>lets-chat needs microphone access to make voice and video calls.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
